%S A401526 8,8,7,6,7,8,2,6,3,7,4,8,2,5,8,0,8,9,2,2,4,2,6,2,5,3,0,3,6,5,8,0,7,6,
%T A401526 3,9,9,3,1,2,1,3,5,2,0,4,9,6,9,0,9,8,3,6,5,8,7,5,3,4,9,0,3,8,5,5,2,9,
%U A401526 8,0,4,7,0,6,2,0,8,2,9,0,4,0,0,1,8,0,3,7,6,4,1,4,8,8,9,9,8,5,3,9,3,6,0,6,3
%N A401526 Decimal expansion of limsup_{N->oo} log(N)*(E - E(N)), where E is the Shannon entropy of Benford's law and E(N) is the Shannon entropy of the leading decimal digit of a random integer k in 1..N chosen with probability proportional to 1/k.
%C A401526 See A401525 for P_d(N), p(d) and E(N), and A400058 for E; E(N) < E for every N >= 1 (Khomyakov, Theorem 3.1(iii)).
%C A401526 log(N)*(E - E(N)) oscillates between A401525 = 0.23401544585... (its liminf, the limit along N = 10^K) and this constant (its limsup, the limit along N = 3*10^K) (Khomyakov, Theorem 4.3(b) and Corollary 4.4).
%C A401526 More precisely, log(N)*(E - E(N)) = D(N/10^K) + O(1/K) with K = floor(log_10(N)), where D is a continuous function on [1,10] with D(1) = D(10) = A401525, whose maximum is attained at an integer in 2..9. By a computer-assisted comparison in interval arithmetic, the maximum is attained only at 3; the next largest value is D(4) = 0.8627941620106... .
%H A401526 R. L. Duncan, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00150517.1969.12431129">Note on the initial digit problem</a>, Fibonacci Quart. 7 (1969), 474-475.
%H A401526 Vladimir Khomyakov, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23660786">The harmonic leading-digit law is one-sided at every N</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A401526 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benford%27s_law">Benford's law</a>.
%F A401526 Equals Sum_{d=1..9} (R(d) + c(d) - (gamma + log(3))*p(d))*log(p(d)), where c(1) = log(2), c(2) = log(3/2), c(d) = 0 for d >= 3, and p(d), R(d) are as in A401525.
%F A401526 Equals A401525 + log(3)*E + log(2)*log(log_10(2)) + log(3/2)*log(log_10(3/2)), where E = A400058 = -Sum_{d=1..9} p(d)*log(p(d)) = 1.99343315079... .
%e A401526 0.887678263748258089224262530365807639931213520496909836587534...
%o A401526 (Python) # via mpmath; D = Sum_{d=1..9} (R(d) + c(d) - (gamma + log(3))*p(d))*log(p(d)), c(1) = log(2), c(2) = log(3/2), c(d) = 0 for d >= 3
%o A401526 from mpmath import mp, mpf, log, digamma, euler
%o A401526 digits = 105          # number of digits to compute; the only parameter to change
%o A401526 mp.dps = digits + 20  # working precision: 20 guard digits
%o A401526 J = digits + 20       # number of terms of R(d): 0 <= R(d) - (sum over j < J) < 10^-J
%o A401526 def p(d): return log(1 + mpf(1)/d)/log(10)
%o A401526 def R(d): return sum(digamma(mpf(d+1)*10**j) - digamma(mpf(d)*10**j) - log(1 + mpf(1)/d) for j in range(J))
%o A401526 c = {1: log(2), 2: log(mpf(3)/2)}
%o A401526 D = sum((R(d) + c.get(d, 0) - (euler + log(3))*p(d))*log(p(d)) for d in range(1, 10))
%o A401526 print(", ".join(str(int(D*10**digits))))  # first `digits` digits of D = 0.8876..., truncated
%Y A401526 Cf. A400058 (E), A401525 (liminf), A401527, A001620, A007524.
%K A401526 nonn,cons,new
%O A401526 0,1
%A A401526 _Vladimir Khomyakov_, Oct 28 2026
